  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The most consequential development in the past 48 hours was the US military's May 25 "self-defense strikes" on Iranian missile launch sites and mine-laying boats near Bandar Abbas, conducted while Iranian negotiators Ghalibaf and Araghchi were simultaneously in Doha for Qatari-mediated peace talks (NBC News, CNBC, Washington Post, 25 May). CENTCOM described the strikes as force-protection measures under the existing ceasefire; Iranian state-linked media labelled them ceasefire violations. The timing — strikes and diplomacy running in parallel on the same day — documents the structural contradiction of US military pressure alongside negotiating engagement, and the targeted mine-laying boats are the precise vessels whose removal is a physical precondition for any Hormuz reopening under the draft MOU terms. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The Iran–US MOU framework reached its most publicly advanced form to date: Trump told NPR on May 23 the deal is "largely negotiated" and "will be announced soon"; Axios published the full 60-day framework terms on May 24, including Hormuz reopening with mine-clearance, sanctions waivers for Iranian oil, and a 12–15 year enrichment moratorium (Axios, 24 May; CNN, 24 May). Against these developments: Iran's FM Araghchi reported a "deadlock" on enriched uranium removal at BRICS foreign ministers meetings, with the issue described as "postponed" to later stages (Times of Israel); FM spokesman Baghaei stated "no one can claim that the signing of an agreement is imminent" (PressTV, 23 May); and Austria's intelligence services published a May 26 assessment that Iran is pursuing an active nuclear weapons capability, which Tehran dismissed across all state outlets. The Witkoff–Araghchi direct bilateral indicator remains unobserved — Doha talks ran through Qatari mediation only. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

      <p>In Israeli domestic politics, the Knesset passed a preliminary first reading of a coalition-sponsored dissolution bill 110-0 on May 20 (Al Jazeera, Times of Israel, Haaretz). The bill does not set an election date; it initiates a three-reading legislative process with date-setting delegated to the House Committee after final enactment. Netanyahu is targeting an October 2026 election to maximise war-credit campaigning; Haredi parties push for September. Smotrich remains inside the coalition; no formal departure was announced. The 110-0 vote — the coalition itself sponsoring the bill — is a controlled dissolution mechanism designed to manage election timing, not a loss of parliamentary majority. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>On the Lebanese front, Netanyahu on May 25 ordered the military to "press the pedal even harder" against Hezbollah following US approval for expanded operations, with the IDF striking infrastructure in the Beqaa Valley and the city of Tyre (Times of Israel, Washington Post, ITV, 25 May). Residents began evacuating Beirut's southern suburbs for the first time since the April 16 truce. An IDF soldier died in southern Lebanon on May 24. Neither the US State Department nor the Lebanese government has declared the ceasefire void as of May 26; Pentagon talks remain scheduled for May 29 and June 2-3 in Washington, confirming both governments consider the truce framework operative. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
