      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (22%):</strong> US/Iran strikes enter a ratchet cycle that the Doha diplomatic track cannot absorb; Lebanon ceasefire formally collapses and Hezbollah returns to full operational tempo — derived from ~1-P(F6) with a multiplier for Lebanon front deterioration and continued US strike cadence near the strait.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (63%):</strong> No MOU signed by June 15 but both sides maintain the Doha channel; Hormuz remains effectively closed, oil prices stabilise at elevated war-time levels through summer, and the negotiating track continues without breakthrough — residual probability after escalation and de-escalation paths.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> Calibrated to F1 (Hormuz at 15%) rather than F6 (deal at 38%): a signed MOU requires sequential mine-clearance, carrier return decisions, and insurance market reset that the 15-day horizon constraint makes physically improbable even under the most optimistic signing scenario.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (PressTV, Mehr News, IRNA) spent the 25–26 May cycle constructing a dual frame: Iran as a legitimate sovereign actor conducting war-end diplomacy from a position of strength, and the US as an unreliable partner whose military actions contradict its stated desire for peace. PressTV's headline sequence on May 25 ran "Iran vows not to yield to US pressure in negotiations" followed within hours by "Iran says US talks focus on ending war — not Hormuz management or nuclear issue" — a deliberate narrowing of the deal's scope intended to address domestic hardliner expectations (PressTV, 25 May). → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Doha delegation's composition — Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf alongside FM Araghchi and Central Bank Governor Hemmati — was presented by Mehr News as evidence of an "integrated national team" with unified political authority behind the talks (Mehr News, 25 May). The enriched uranium stockpile issue was framed not as Iranian intransigence but as a US "maximalist demand" incompatible with Iranian sovereignty; Supreme Leader guidance on retaining enriched material was referenced approvingly in some outlets without direct name attribution, a pattern of editorial caution observed when sensitive talks are in progress. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Austria's intelligence report on Iran's nuclear weapons program was dismissed in unanimous terms across all state-linked outlets as a "Zionist-backed propaganda operation" timed to derail negotiations — the attribution of foreign intelligence assessments to coordinated sabotage is a standard IRIB template at sensitive diplomatic junctures. No state media outlet gave the US Bandar Abbas strikes prominent headline treatment; coverage categorised them as minor provocations demonstrating US bad faith rather than substantive military events, consistent with the broader state media project of maintaining an Iranian-initiative framing for the war-end talks. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

